 College\nMission & Vision\nDepartments\nCenters, Institutes, & Research\nCareer Development\nStudent Success\nFacilities & Locations\nStudent Experience & Special Programs\nExplore Our College\nPrograms & Admission\nUndergraduate Programs\nGraduate Programs\nCertificate Programs\nPre-College Summer Program\nHow to Apply & Transfers\nScholarships & Aid\nVisit\nMore in Programs & Admission\nConnect & Contact\nLeadership & Staff\nFaculty Directory\nNews & Events\nAlumni & Giving\nContact Us\nConnect With Us\nREQUEST INFO\nAPPLY\nVISIT\nGIVE\nLet Us Help\nSearch\nSubmit Search\nHome\nExplore Our College\nDepartments\nDepartments\nIn This Section\nIn This Section\nExplore Our College\nMission & Vision\nThe Current Page is Departments\nDepartment of Biomedical Engineering\nDepartment of Civil and Environmental Engineering\nDepartment of Computer Science\nDepartment of Electrical and Computer Engineering\nDepartment of Mechanical Engineering\nDepartment of Physics\nCenters, Institutes, & Research\nCareer Development\nStudent Success\nFacilities & Locations\nStudent Experience & Special Programs\nThe College of Engineering, Physics, and Computing is home to 6 academic departments where creativity, science, and technology converge.\nDepartment of Biomedical Engineering\nBiomedical engineering aims to improve human healthcare, diagnostics, and patient treatment by applying engineering principles and technology to biology and medicine.\nAbout the Department\nDepartment of Civil and Environmental Engineering\nCivil and environmental engineering focuses on designing, building, and maintaining essential infrastructure like roads, bridges, dams, water systems, and buildings. The discipline emphasizes safety, efficiency, and environmental sustainability.\nAbout the Department\nDepartment of Computer Science\nComputer science is the study of computers, computational systems, and algorithmic processes to understand how technology solves complex problems. Its core elements of study include algorithms, data structures, programming languages, and computational theory.\nAbout the Department\nDepartment of Electrical and Computer Engineering\nThe field of electrical and computer engineering integrates hardware and software to design, develop, and optimize advanced technologies that range from microscopic circuits to massive power grids. It combines principles of electricity, electronics, and electromagnetism with computer science to create systems like smartphones, robots, and autonomous vehicles.\nAbout the Department\nDepartment of Mechanical Engineering\nMechanical engineering is a broad field that applies principles of physics, mathematics, and material science to design, analyze, manufacture, and maintain mechanical systems, machines, and energy tools. Mechanical engineers work on anything that moves, including engines, robotics, HVAC systems, and aerospace technologies.\nAbout the Department\nDepartment of Physics\nPhysics is the fundamental natural science that aims to understand how the universe behaves by studying matter, energy, space, time, and their interactions. It explores everything from subatomic particles to galaxies, using concepts like force, motion, and thermodynamics.\nAbout the Department\nCollege of Engineering, Physics, and Computing\nPangborn Hall 334\nThe Catholic University of America\n620 Michigan Ave., NE\nWashington, DC 20064\nFacebook (Meta)\nInstagram\nLinkedIn\nX (Formerly known as Twitter)\nSupport the College\nMake a gift to Catholic University\nAbout\nDepartments\nHow to Apply\nAccreditation\nContact\nResources & Support\nFor Current Students\nFor Faculty & Staff\nEmployment\nAthletics\nCardinal Shop\nLibraries\nDirectory\nCareers\nFor Media\nCopyright & Trademark\nPrivacy\nConsumer Information\nEn Espa\u00f1ol\nDisability Support\nReport Misconduct\nCompliance\nFacebook\nInstagram\nYouTube\nLinkedIn\n\u00a9 1887-2026\nThe Catholic University of America", "links": [["Skip to main content", "https://engineering.ca